[REQUIREMENT]
UID: MEC-007
TITLE: Magnetic and material property traceability
STATEMENT: >>>
Every magnetic material, potting compound and thermal interface material shall
have its relevant properties recorded in ``docs/reference/manifest.yaml`` against
a datasheet revision or a measurement made in this programme.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
The mechanical half of ``SYS-010``. Ferrite permeability and loss at 85 kHz, and
thermal interface conductivity, are exactly the numbers that get taken from
memory and are exactly the ones the thermal and magnetic designs rest on. The v1
white paper names ferrite backing without naming a material at all.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-010
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: MEC-009
TITLE: Interface requirement - the mounting structure is the heat sink
STATEMENT: >>>
The structure each pad mounts to - the deck fixture, the vehicle mounting and
the test packaging alike - shall conduct the full pad dissipation of ``MEC-004``
into free air with at least 12 times the pad footprint in external surface area,
and shall hold the coil conductor below 100 degC in the ``SYS-016`` ambient with
no pumped coolant and no forced air. This programme shall state this as a
numbered interface requirement to whoever builds that structure, and shall meet
it in the test packaging.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
This requirement exists because the pad cannot cool itself and the arithmetic is
not close. A flat plate in still air sheds roughly 150 W per square metre at an
acceptable temperature rise, so the 207 square centimetre pad sheds about 3.1 W
from its own faces. The design has to remove about 36 W. The pad face is
therefore irrelevant to the thermal problem and every watt leaves through the
bracket, which needs roughly 2400 square centimetres of external surface - about
twelve times the pad footprint.

A finned aluminium plate of about 400 by 500 mm provides that comfortably, which
is exactly why the decision to charge on deck in a fixture is what makes an
etched coil viable at all. A hull-mounted pad with only its own faces to work
with could not have been cooled by any coil technology.

The fixture became the mechanical team's on 2026-08-31 (``VIS-013``). This
requirement was NOT withdrawn with it, and the distinction is the whole point:
we no longer design the bracket, but the coil we do design is only viable
because something cools it, so the surface area becomes a number we levy on the
integrator rather than a number we satisfy ourselves. Handing over a coil
without handing over its heat sink requirement is how a design that closed on
paper arrives at a 90 degC pad. The test packaging must meet it too, because a
thermal measurement taken on a rig with a smaller heat sink would understate the
temperature the delivered system runs at.

The 12 times figure is researched but NOT independently verified - the
verification pass on the thermal topic did not run - so it must be confirmed
against a second source before this requirement is closed. The concrete warning
behind it is measured: a published fully transposed multi-track PCB coil at
3.3 kW reached 90.3 degC and was still rising when power was cut at 33 minutes.
See ``docs/design/adr-0001-coil-technology.md``.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Simulation
STATUS: Draft
RELATIONS:
- TYPE: Parent
  VALUE: SYS-007
  ROLE: Refines

[REQUIREMENT]
UID: MEC-010
TITLE: Test packaging construction and pin locating
STATEMENT: >>>
The test packaging shall locate each coil assembly on two dowel pins in a
hole-and-slot pattern, shall set the gap of ``SYS-017`` with shims or a
graduated adjuster readable to 0.5 mm, and shall be producible by 3-axis
machining and sheet fabrication from stock material with no programme-specific
tooling.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Hole-and-slot on two pins is the standard way to fix a plate in six degrees of
freedom without over-constraining it: the round hole fixes position, the slot
fixes rotation and leaves the pin-spacing tolerance and thermal growth somewhere
to go. Locating on two round holes instead is the common mistake - it fights the
pin spacing and either binds or has to be opened up until it no longer locates.

Shims set the gap rather than a screw thread because a shim stack is directly
measurable with the same gauge that verifies it, and ``SYS-017`` needs the gap
KNOWN to 0.5 mm rather than merely adjustable.

The producibility clause mirrors ``MEC-006``. It is here rather than inherited
because the test packaging is test equipment and would otherwise sit outside
that requirement's scope, and a rig that needs its own tooling does not get
built in time to verify anything.
<<<
VERIFICATION: Inspection
STATUS: Draft
